# 6. DOCUMENT EXTRACTION AND VALIDATION ENGINE

Implement deterministic, reusable validation logic in a dedicated module.

Do not make the core functionality dependent on paid AI APIs.

## A. Required-field validation

Check whether required application fields are missing or invalid.

## B. Document completeness

Compare the uploaded document categories against the checklist configured for the selected application category.

Identify:
- Required documents that are missing.
- Documents with incomplete metadata.
- Documents uploaded under an incompatible category.
- Items that cannot be evaluated with the available information.

## C. Expiry validation

When a document has a known expiry date, compare it with the selected application date or a clearly defined reference date.

Distinguish:
- Valid on the reference date.
- Expiring soon.
- Expired.
- Expiry date unavailable.

Do not invent expiry dates or silently assume a certificate is valid when its dates cannot be established.

## D. Cross-document consistency

Where information is available, compare:

- Institution name.
- Institution code.
- Certificate number.
- Issue date.
- Expiry date.
- Application reference.
- Programme and intake information.

Flag mismatches with both observed and expected values when the expected value has a reliable source.

Do not assume that every document contains every field.

## E. Duplicate-file detection

Calculate SHA-256 hashes for uploaded files where supported.

Identify exact duplicate files and display the names of the matching documents.

Keep duplicate detection separate from conclusions about document authenticity.

## F. Document anomaly indicators

Create explainable, deterministic checks for:

- Conflicting dates.
- Mismatched identifiers.
- Inconsistent values across records.
- Suspicious duplicate submissions.
- Missing expected metadata.
- Other demonstrable anomalies.

If actual document-forgery detection is not implemented, explicitly describe this module as preliminary anomaly screening.

Do not claim to detect sophisticated document manipulation reliably through simple metadata heuristics.

## G. OCR and text extraction

Use an available, compatible library for PDF text extraction where feasible.

For scanned documents or images, implement actual OCR only if a compatible local or free implementation is practical in the current environment.

If extraction is unavailable or fails:
- Show "Text extraction unavailable" or "Extraction failed".
- Allow manual metadata entry.
- Offer a clearly labeled demonstration-extraction mode where appropriate.
- Never display invented extracted text as though it came from the uploaded document.

Keep extracted text, manually entered data and synthetic demo values clearly distinguishable.

## H. Validation findings

Each finding must contain:

- Finding ID.
- Check name.
- Status.
- Severity.
- Description.
- Reason for the finding.
- Affected document or application field.
- Supporting evidence.
- Expected and observed values when applicable.
- Recommended next action.
- Human-review requirement.

Use understandable status labels:

- Passed
- Attention Required
- Action Required
- Verification Pending
- Manual Review Required

Use severity levels such as Low, Medium and High.

A high-severity flag must not automatically establish wrongdoing.

